From: CachyOS-ARM64 port overlay <builder@local>
Date: Wed, 08 Oct 2026
Subject: [PATCH] remoteproc: drop unreachable RPROC_DELETED check

rproc_boot() checked `rproc->state == RPROC_DELETED`, but no such state
exists anywhere in this tree: enum rproc_state is complete and consecutive
(OFFLINE=0, SUSPENDED=1, RUNNING=2, CRASHED=3, ATTACHED=4, DETACHED=5,
LAST=6), and a full-tree grep finds exactly one occurrence of the token --
this check. No code ever assigns RPROC_DELETED, so the guard is
unreachable. It looks like a leftover from a partially rebased
Ubuntu/laptops-kernel patch that also lost its enum addition.

Removing the dead block preserves behavior exactly and fixes the build:

  drivers/remoteproc/remoteproc_core.c:1960:29: error: 'RPROC_DELETED'
  undeclared (first use in this function)

If the missing deletion path is ever restored upstream, the guard should
return with it (and the enum + rproc_state_string table must stay
synchronized as the header documents).
---
 drivers/remoteproc/remoteproc_core.c | 5 -----
 1 file changed, 5 deletions(-)

diff --git a/drivers/remoteproc/remoteproc_core.c b/drivers/remoteproc/remoteproc_core.c
--- a/drivers/remoteproc/remoteproc_core.c
+++ b/drivers/remoteproc/remoteproc_core.c
@@ -1957,11 +1957,6 @@ int rproc_boot(struct rproc *rproc)
 		return ret;
 	}
 
-	if (rproc->state == RPROC_DELETED) {
-		ret = -ENODEV;
-		dev_err(dev, "can't boot deleted rproc %s\n", rproc->name);
-		goto unlock_mutex;
-	}
-
 	/* skip the boot or attach process if rproc is already powered up */
 	if (atomic_inc_return(&rproc->power) > 1) {
 		ret = 0;
